<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/unification-of-italy-and-germany">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1848, Germans and Italians had tried to build nations out of barricades and had lost.
    Within twenty-three years both existed anyway — built by statesmen, soldiers and
    plebiscites instead. These two lessons answer Cambridge’s Option A key questions
    <strong>2 and 3</strong>: <em>How was Italy unified?</em> and <em>How was Germany unified?</em>
    Watch the two clocks run side by side, then examine the two engines: Cavour and Garibaldi
    for Italy, Bismarck’s three short wars for Germany.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two unifications, one timeline — filter by nation with the chips" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>Italy: the prophet, the statesman, the soldier</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mazzini</strong> supplied the idea: a nation grows from its awakened people, and
    Young Italy’s conspiracies kept the flame alive through the 1830s and 1840s — but the
    Roman Republic of 1849, crushed by French troops, proved that idealism without an army
    fails. <strong>Cavour</strong> supplied the method: make Piedmont-Sardinia so modern and
    respectable that Europe backs it. He sent Piedmontese troops to the Crimean War in 1855 to
    buy a seat at the peace table, met Napoleon III at Plombières in 1858, and fought Austria
    with him in 1859 — winning Lombardy at Magenta and Solferino, though Villafranca’s peace
    left Venetia Austrian and showed that great-power allies keep their own diary. The central
    duchies joined Piedmont by plebiscite in March 1860 (France’s price: Nice and Savoy).
    <strong>Garibaldi</strong> supplied the drama: his Thousand took Sicily and Naples in five
    months, and his decision at Teano to hand the south to Victor Emmanuel II — rather than
    march on Rome as a revolutionary — fused peninsula and kingdom. The Kingdom of Italy was
    proclaimed on 17 March 1861; Venetia came in 1866 as the spoils of Prussia’s victory
    (despite Italy losing at Custoza and Lissa), and Rome in September 1870 when France could
    no longer guard the Pope.
  </p>

  <h2>Germany: Bismarck’s three wars</h2>
  <p>
    Germany’s preconditions differed: no single state dominated, but the
    <strong>Zollverein</strong> (1834) had already made Prussia the economic centre of gravity,
    with Austria deliberately outside. The Frankfurt Parliament’s failure and Frederick William
    IV’s refusal of the crown (1849), followed by the Humiliation of Olmütz (1850), had
    re-established Austrian leadership — so Bismarck, Minister-President from September 1862,
    set out to reverse it. His method was always the same: <strong>isolate the victim, then
    strike</strong>. Against Denmark (1864) he fought <em>with</em> Austria, winning
    Schleswig-Holstein and a shared administration built for quarrelling. Against Austria
    (1866) he bought Russia’s restraint, allied Italy, and let Moltke win at Königgrätz in
    seven weeks — then made a lenient peace, annexing only the north-German middling states
    and founding the North German Confederation (1867), so Austria would stand aside next
    time. Against France (1870) the Hohenzollern candidature for Spain’s throne and the edited
    Ems telegram baited Napoleon III into declaring war; Sedan destroyed his army, and on
    18 January 1871 the German Empire was proclaimed in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles.
    Bismarck then took Alsace-Lorraine — the mistake that poisoned the peace he had made.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bismarck’s real constraint was arithmetic, not diplomacy: Prussia could not be strong
    everywhere at once. Drag the arrow to feel the problem:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="interior lines: point Prussia’s army at one front and the other goes thin" widget="diagramBuilder" config={frontsCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the axes as <strong>x = force massed west against France, y = force massed
    south-east against Austria</strong>. The starting arrow, pointing mostly east, is the 1866
    posture: the main mass against Austria, a thin screen on the Rhine — safe only because
    Bismarck had bought French goodwill. Drag it mostly west for 1870: the main mass against
    France, the east exposed — safe only because Austria had been beaten <em>and leniently
    treated</em> in 1866. Now try making both components 10: the magnitude readout shows 14 —
    no Prussia possessed that army. Unification was the art of never having to point the arrow
    both ways at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Same destination, different roads</h2>
  <p>
    Italy was unified by an untidy coalition of diplomacy, volunteers and plebiscites, needing
    foreign arms twice (France in 1859, Prussia in 1866); Germany by three calculated wars
    launched from above. Germany’s unification settled the great 1848 question —
    <em>Großdeutsch</em> with Austria or <em>kleindeutsch</em> without — by exclusion. Both
    new states left unfinished business that shaped the syllabus’s next key questions:
    Italian “unredeemed” lands in Trento and Trieste, and above all Alsace-Lorraine, which
    turned Franco-German rivalry into the fault line of 1914. Unification also asked the
    focus-point question of other nations: France lost a European ally in Italy’s priests’
    protectorate and gained the raw enmity of a Germany that now outmatched it — by 1871 the
    balance of power had a new hegemon.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Core Option A key questions 2–3; Hearder, Italy in the Age of the Risorgimento 1790–1900; Pflanze, Bismarck and the Development of Germany.</p>
